 [17592186172928 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "213cee54-dfad-5696-a210-97d588e61069" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172928 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186173170 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173151 #edb/ref 1064 "When finding the roots of a transformed radical function, we need to consider whether the radical's index is odd or even.\n\nLet's start with the odd case first. As an example, we will find the root of the function $f(x)$, given by\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\sqrt[{\\color{blue}{3}}]{10-4x} + 2\\,\n$$\n\nNotice that we're dealing with a cube root, and so the index of the radical is ${\\color{blue}{3}}$, which is odd.\n\nTo find the root of $f(x)$, we must solve\n\n$$\nf(x)=0\n$$\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{10 - 4x} + 2 = 0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt[3]{10 - 4x} + 2 &= 0 \\\\\n\\sqrt[3]{10 - 4x} &= -2 \\\\\n(\\sqrt[3]{10 - 4x})^{3} &= (-2)^{3} \\\\\n10 - 4x &= -8 \\\\\n- 4x &= -18 \\\\\nx &= \\frac{9}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac92\n$$\n\nA transformed radical function with an odd index will always have one real root." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173151 #edb/ref 1065 4864 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173151 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a49268e2-e58c-5966-ba98-b1786827ba76" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173151 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173170 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's now learn how to find the root of a radical function with an even index. For example, let's try to find the root of\n\n$$\nf(x) = -3\\sqrt[4]{10x+1} + 9\n$$\n\nTo find the root of $f(x)$, we must solve\n\n$$\nf(x)=0\n$$\n\n$$\n-3\\sqrt[4]{10x+1} + 9 = 0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-3\\sqrt[4]{10x + 1} + 9 &= 0 \\\\\n- 3\\sqrt[4]{10x + 1} &= -9 \\\\\n\\sqrt[4]{10x + 1} &= 3 \\\\\n(\\sqrt[4]{10x + 1})^{4} &= 3^{4} \\\\\n10x + 1 &= 81 \\\\\n10x &= 80 \\\\\nx &= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we must check the answer by substituting it back into the initial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-3\\sqrt[4]{10 \\cdot 8 + 1} + 9 &= 0 \\\\\n- 3\\sqrt[4]{81} + 9 &= 0 \\\\\n- 3 \\cdot 3 + 9 &= 0 \\\\\n0 &= 0✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=8$.\n\nWhen the index of the radical function is even, we should always check for extraneous roots by substituting them back into the original equation.\n\nSometimes, a radical function with an even index has no root. Let's see an example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173170 #edb/ref 1065 5161 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173170 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0a1feb60-62da-5279-8bfd-f8be6c389de7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173170 #edb/ref 1067 "Finding the Roots of a Transformed Radical Function: Even Case" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235818 #edb/ref 1080 "x=-\\frac{13}{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235818 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235818 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "03873688-2ce0-5a7d-a270-7a389651c5cd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186236021 #edb/ref 1080 "The function has no roots" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186236021 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186236021 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1fdea023-ee70-5e48-80b9-a4af404acfb0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186236249 #edb/ref 1080 "x=4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186236249 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186236249 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4316944c-e18a-5667-8357-3240d5834ee1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186236387 #edb/ref 1080 "x=1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186236387 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186236387 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5b7aa047-300c-51e2-a7f6-c86c3378272d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186236661 #edb/ref 1080 "x=3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186236661 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186236661 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8767245b-bfc6-5e